    "body": "## Overview\r\n\r\nWith sliding window groups, the OffloadingConnector applied its prompt-end `-1` after the `max_load_tokens` / `max_num_new_tokens` caps, so a whole-chunk cap lost a chunk (a one-chunk cap loaded nothing). Follow-up to #55885.\r\n\r\n## Claims\r\n\r\n- With sliding window groups (and no partial-tail support, e.g. gpt-oss, Gemma 2/3), `max_load_tokens = k * tokens_per_chunk` now loads `k` chunks instead of `k - 1`.\r\n- The same lost chunk is fixed for `max_num_new_tokens` (the bound from groups the connector does not offload).\r\n- No change when there is no cap: the hit still stops before the last prompt token.\r\n\r\n## Validation\r\n\r\nNew test `test_max_load_tokens_preserves_aligned_sliding_window_chunk` (16-token chunks, 65-token prompt, mixed full/sliding-window groups):\r\n\r\n| Cap | Before | After |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| 16 tokens | 0 | 16 |\r\n| 32 tokens | 16 | 32 |\r\n| none | 64 | 64 |\r\n\r\n```bash\r\npytest tests/v1/kv_connector/unit/offloading_connector/test_scheduler.py \\\r\n  -k \"max_load_tokens or sliding\"   # 132 passed\r\npytest tests/v1/kv_connector/unit/offloading_connector\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Details\r\n\r\n`_lookup_complete_chunks` computed `min(num_tokens, computed + cap) - 1`. The `-1` exists so a sliding-window hit stops before the last prompt token, which must be recomputed. When a cap ends earlier, it is already a supported boundary, but the `-1` moved it one token below, and the per-group `round_down` then dropped a whole chunk. The fix applies the `-1` to the prompt-end ceiling only: `min(num_tokens - 1, computed + cap)`. This matches the partial-tail path, which already uses `num_prompt_tokens - 1` with uncapped limits. Mamba alignment is unchanged.\r\n\r\nNo existing issue or PR covers this (searched `max_load_tokens`, `max_hit_size_tokens`, sliding window + offloading). Draft #62932 edits nearby lines for a different purpose (capacity-bounded loads); expect a trivial rebase for whichever lands second.\r\n\r\nTest creation and validation assisted by Claude Code.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\r\n\r\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\r\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\r\n\r\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\r\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\r\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\r\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n",
